# Two-Voice Invention for Solo Guitar (repertoire plan: st-two-voice-invention). An original
# 24-bar invention in E minor for one guitar, in the manner of the Baroque two-part inventions.
# The subject (bar 1, treble alone): E up to B, A G F# E running down, D# B, home to E.
# Bar 2: the bass imitates it an octave lower while the treble plays the countersubject
# (G | C A | B A G F# | G E). Bars 3-6: episodes built from the subject's running figure,
# cadencing in E minor (bar 4) and turning towards G major. Bars 7-10: G major; the subject in
# the bass (bar 9), then in the treble (bar 10). Bars 11-16: back to E minor over a falling bass,
# ending on a pedal on low B. Bar 17: the subject in E minor in the treble with the
# countersubject below it (the two lines swap places, and every interval still works); bar 18
# and bar 21 put it in the bass again; bar 23 states it over the open low E. It ends on E major.
# Positions: VII (finger 1 on fret 7) for most of it; V (finger 1 on fret 5) in bars 5-6,
# 11-12 and 22; open position for the last chord. A held note is written as a tie (string:-):
# keep that finger down so the note lasts its full length while the other voice moves.
# The chord symbols name the harmony the two lines make; they are not strummed.
title: Two-Voice Invention for Solo Guitar
composer: Sixstring Academy
source: Original two-part invention for solo guitar
licence: Original study for Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 9
